WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.617 --> 00:00:06.450
BRIAN DORSEY: Hello.

00:00:06.450 --> 00:00:08.500
I'm Brian, and we're
here to talk about--

00:00:08.500 --> 00:00:10.730
where should I run my stuff?

00:00:10.730 --> 00:00:15.020
So like all good
questions in technology,

00:00:15.020 --> 00:00:17.970
the answer is, it depends.

00:00:17.970 --> 00:00:21.288
And that's the reason
there's this whole talk.

00:00:21.288 --> 00:00:23.330
So we're going to talk
through what it depends on

00:00:23.330 --> 00:00:27.890
and how to make these decisions.

00:00:27.890 --> 00:00:31.340
And basically, I'm going to
go through each of these,

00:00:31.340 --> 00:00:35.950
except Firebase, and
talk about what they are,

00:00:35.950 --> 00:00:40.070
what their sweet spots are, and
what the trade-offs are, like

00:00:40.070 --> 00:00:43.070
how you might make a
decision between them,

00:00:43.070 --> 00:00:46.280
and then some more kind
of bonus stuff around

00:00:46.280 --> 00:00:48.660
how they can work together.

00:00:48.660 --> 00:00:52.640
So each of these
I think of as part

00:00:52.640 --> 00:00:55.070
of an abstraction layered cake.

00:00:55.070 --> 00:00:57.650
So at the top, things are more
abstract, and at the bottom,

00:00:57.650 --> 00:00:58.700
they're more concrete.

00:00:58.700 --> 00:01:01.670
So the bottom, we
have VMs, right?

00:01:01.670 --> 00:01:05.728
At the top, Firebase is a tool
for people writing mobile apps

00:01:05.728 --> 00:01:07.520
and web apps where they
don't want to think

00:01:07.520 --> 00:01:10.010
about the back end at all.

00:01:10.010 --> 00:01:12.580
So it's kind of all
handled for you.

00:01:12.580 --> 00:01:15.160
Down one level is
Cloud Functions,

00:01:15.160 --> 00:01:17.110
and it's a really
good fit for things

00:01:17.110 --> 00:01:21.220
that are primarily event
driven, or perhaps kind of glue.

00:01:21.220 --> 00:01:23.800
Next down, we have
App Engine, and it's

00:01:23.800 --> 00:01:28.590
for thinking about an
entire web app altogether.

00:01:28.590 --> 00:01:33.450
Next one down combines web apps
and events with containers.

00:01:33.450 --> 00:01:36.060
So you can run anything
that runs in a container

00:01:36.060 --> 00:01:39.750
and serve it up on
the web with no fuss.

00:01:39.750 --> 00:01:41.760
Next level down is
Kubernetes Engine,

00:01:41.760 --> 00:01:44.400
which is hosted
Kubernetes for running

00:01:44.400 --> 00:01:46.560
whole clusters of
machines running

00:01:46.560 --> 00:01:48.270
an application, or
multiple applications,

00:01:48.270 --> 00:01:50.380
with lots of containers.

00:01:50.380 --> 00:01:53.170
And then next level below
that is straight VMs--

00:01:53.170 --> 00:01:54.340
virtual machines.

00:01:54.340 --> 00:01:58.660
Run anything you want to
that runs on a computer.

00:01:58.660 --> 00:02:00.890
And by the end of
this, I'm going

00:02:00.890 --> 00:02:04.570
to basically try to convince
you that this is a comparatively

00:02:04.570 --> 00:02:06.430
low risk decision.

00:02:06.430 --> 00:02:10.919
Don't worry too much about the
right or wrong choices here,

00:02:10.919 --> 00:02:13.650
but still give you good
info on where to start.

00:02:13.650 --> 00:02:16.380
So let's go through this,
and we'll start kind of top

00:02:16.380 --> 00:02:20.640
to bottom, and start
with Cloud Functions.

00:02:20.640 --> 00:02:25.100
So Cloud Functions are a
function, right, in the cloud.

00:02:25.100 --> 00:02:26.750
I guess the name makes sense.

00:02:26.750 --> 00:02:30.020
And it's basically you
write the function that

00:02:30.020 --> 00:02:34.520
takes some parameters in,
perhaps has some side effects,

00:02:34.520 --> 00:02:38.520
and returns a value, and
we handle all the rest.

00:02:38.520 --> 00:02:41.330
And you write that into one of
the few programming languages.

00:02:41.330 --> 00:02:44.570
And these functions
are triggered

00:02:44.570 --> 00:02:47.420
by an event happening
somewhere else in the system,

00:02:47.420 --> 00:02:50.090
either coming in from the
web, or from our message

00:02:50.090 --> 00:02:52.100
through Pub/Sub, or from
Cloud Storage, which

00:02:52.100 --> 00:02:54.650
is our object store.

00:02:54.650 --> 00:02:56.550
So something happens
in the world,

00:02:56.550 --> 00:02:58.490
and then your
function gets called.

00:02:58.490 --> 00:03:01.070
That's the high
level model there.

00:03:01.070 --> 00:03:03.230
And all the rest of
the infrastructure

00:03:03.230 --> 00:03:04.790
is managed for you.

00:03:04.790 --> 00:03:08.190
You pay for what you use, and
it auto scales up and down.

00:03:08.190 --> 00:03:10.490
So as a developer,
you're thinking

00:03:10.490 --> 00:03:12.710
about what events
make up your system,

00:03:12.710 --> 00:03:17.090
and what the definitions
of the functions are.

00:03:17.090 --> 00:03:19.540
So this is a really good
fit for serverless, right?

00:03:19.540 --> 00:03:23.600
It almost kind of fits the
definition of serverless.

00:03:23.600 --> 00:03:26.900
It is a really good
choice when you've

00:03:26.900 --> 00:03:32.420
got Pub/Sub or Cloud Storage as
a key part of your application.

00:03:32.420 --> 00:03:34.150
Anytime you really
don't want to think

00:03:34.150 --> 00:03:36.700
about the runtime environment
at all, just how the pieces

00:03:36.700 --> 00:03:40.150
connect, it's a good fit.

00:03:40.150 --> 00:03:42.718
Data transformation-- people
don't necessarily think of that

00:03:42.718 --> 00:03:45.010
as a top level thing, but
you've got information coming

00:03:45.010 --> 00:03:47.468
in on a regular basis and you
need to make it in a slightly

00:03:47.468 --> 00:03:48.250
different format--

00:03:48.250 --> 00:03:49.580
that sort of thing.

00:03:49.580 --> 00:03:51.560
And my favorite is that
I just think about it

00:03:51.560 --> 00:03:53.250
as kind of web glue.

00:03:53.250 --> 00:03:55.000
So we've got all these
different APIs that

00:03:55.000 --> 00:03:56.750
help you do lots
of different stuff,

00:03:56.750 --> 00:03:58.278
and sometimes all
you need to make

00:03:58.278 --> 00:04:00.070
a system is to connect
two or three of them

00:04:00.070 --> 00:04:03.370
together in a particular
way, and boom--

00:04:03.370 --> 00:04:04.520
you've got some real value.

00:04:04.520 --> 00:04:07.930
So it's a good fit for
that sort of thing.

00:04:07.930 --> 00:04:10.180
On the constraint
side, since you're

00:04:10.180 --> 00:04:13.240
thinking about functions all
the time, It's only functions.

00:04:13.240 --> 00:04:15.087
And when you start
having lots of them,

00:04:15.087 --> 00:04:17.170
it can be a little bit
challenging to kind of keep

00:04:17.170 --> 00:04:18.640
track of them, or debug--

00:04:18.640 --> 00:04:21.040
that sort of thing.

00:04:21.040 --> 00:04:22.727
All of the interaction
is via events,

00:04:22.727 --> 00:04:24.310
so if you want to
start them yourself,

00:04:24.310 --> 00:04:27.070
you have to come up with some
other way to kind of poke it.

00:04:27.070 --> 00:04:31.750
And we support certain
specific language run times.

00:04:31.750 --> 00:04:35.360
Pretty good variety, but if it
matches what you need, great.

00:04:35.360 --> 00:04:38.800
If it doesn't, you may have
to look somewhere else.

00:04:38.800 --> 00:04:39.580
OK.

00:04:39.580 --> 00:04:42.220
Next level down is App Engine.

00:04:42.220 --> 00:04:45.370
So App Engine is arguably--

00:04:45.370 --> 00:04:50.140
it is the earliest
Cloud thing that Google

00:04:50.140 --> 00:04:54.670
did as a developer
product before we even

00:04:54.670 --> 00:04:57.550
had the name Google Cloud.

00:04:57.550 --> 00:05:01.100
And its goal is to really
help developers just focus

00:05:01.100 --> 00:05:01.600
on the code.

00:05:01.600 --> 00:05:04.930
So you've got a full code
base, the handles mapping URLs

00:05:04.930 --> 00:05:06.812
to different parts of the code.

00:05:06.812 --> 00:05:08.770
There's a couple different
data stores that you

00:05:08.770 --> 00:05:10.300
can use with it nicely.

00:05:10.300 --> 00:05:12.020
And all the rest of
the infrastructure

00:05:12.020 --> 00:05:12.880
is handled for you.

00:05:12.880 --> 00:05:14.505
So you're thinking
about your whole app

00:05:14.505 --> 00:05:16.690
and all the different
parts of the web--

00:05:16.690 --> 00:05:20.800
URLs that have handlers.

00:05:20.800 --> 00:05:22.720
Since it is a very
mature thing, it

00:05:22.720 --> 00:05:28.000
has deep support for things like
versioning, traffic splitting,

00:05:28.000 --> 00:05:31.270
a lot of granularity in
seeing what's going on

00:05:31.270 --> 00:05:34.150
with it, that sort of thing.

00:05:34.150 --> 00:05:37.930
And I think you could
arguably say it was serverless

00:05:37.930 --> 00:05:43.900
before it was cool, before that
term was really in popular use.

00:05:43.900 --> 00:05:47.030
As a developer, when
you're using App Engine,

00:05:47.030 --> 00:05:49.260
you're really thinking
about the code base,

00:05:49.260 --> 00:05:51.340
you know, all of the code
that makes up your app,

00:05:51.340 --> 00:05:54.370
and the particular web
requests that come in,

00:05:54.370 --> 00:05:57.010
and which parts of the code you
want them to hit, what handler,

00:05:57.010 --> 00:06:00.760
and what you want to do about
that, as the primary things.

00:06:00.760 --> 00:06:03.040
That's where you spend
almost all your time.

00:06:03.040 --> 00:06:06.250
And then, additionally, this
kind of idea of, OK, here's

00:06:06.250 --> 00:06:07.660
a new version of the code.

00:06:07.660 --> 00:06:10.202
I'd like to test that out with
a small amount of the traffic,

00:06:10.202 --> 00:06:15.540
or move forward or
roll back afterwards.

00:06:15.540 --> 00:06:20.060
It's a really good fit for an
application that is HTTPS--

00:06:20.060 --> 00:06:26.900
HTTP or HTTPS web driven app,
for things that are primarily

00:06:26.900 --> 00:06:30.830
stateless or use one of
our managed databases,

00:06:30.830 --> 00:06:33.890
and especially
for scaling really

00:06:33.890 --> 00:06:38.160
quickly from perhaps very
low levels of traffic

00:06:38.160 --> 00:06:40.500
to very high levels of traffic.

00:06:40.500 --> 00:06:42.060
One of the sweet
spot use cases is

00:06:42.060 --> 00:06:46.440
things like landing pages
for seasonal events or things

00:06:46.440 --> 00:06:48.450
like that where it
doesn't get really used

00:06:48.450 --> 00:06:51.980
for most of the year, and
then suddenly super busy.

00:06:51.980 --> 00:06:56.730
On the constraint side, again,
we have certain language

00:06:56.730 --> 00:06:59.260
runtimes that are fully
supported-- things like Java,

00:06:59.260 --> 00:07:06.120
PHP, Python, Go, Node, and
you really are speaking web

00:07:06.120 --> 00:07:06.990
requests--

00:07:06.990 --> 00:07:08.670
like the request, response.

00:07:08.670 --> 00:07:12.010
That's how you have
to talk to App Engine.

00:07:12.010 --> 00:07:15.310
Next level down the
abstraction tree is Cloud Run,

00:07:15.310 --> 00:07:20.030
and this is where serverless can
really connect with containers.

00:07:20.030 --> 00:07:21.950
And so what's a container?

00:07:21.950 --> 00:07:23.387
Why containers?

00:07:23.387 --> 00:07:25.720
Could go into a lot of depth
here, but concretely, we're

00:07:25.720 --> 00:07:27.370
talking docker containers.

00:07:27.370 --> 00:07:31.480
And I think of them as really
solving the problem you have

00:07:31.480 --> 00:07:35.410
where the software works one
way on your dev environment,

00:07:35.410 --> 00:07:36.750
but doesn't work in production.

00:07:36.750 --> 00:07:39.128
You know, like, well,
worked on my machine--

00:07:39.128 --> 00:07:39.920
that sort of thing.

00:07:39.920 --> 00:07:43.870
So basically, they wrap
up the program, all

00:07:43.870 --> 00:07:45.218
of the files it needs to run--

00:07:45.218 --> 00:07:47.260
maybe some data files and
some stuff-- into a zip

00:07:47.260 --> 00:07:49.990
file that gets pushed
to a repository,

00:07:49.990 --> 00:07:52.450
and then that gets pulled down.

00:07:52.450 --> 00:07:54.400
And every single time
the program's run,

00:07:54.400 --> 00:07:56.330
it sees the same initial state.

00:07:56.330 --> 00:07:59.000
So it kind of
solves that problem.

00:07:59.000 --> 00:08:00.490
Containers are lovely.

00:08:00.490 --> 00:08:04.500
They let you run almost
any software in them.

00:08:04.500 --> 00:08:06.910
And Cloud Run is a
way to take anything

00:08:06.910 --> 00:08:09.370
that does run in a
container on Linux

00:08:09.370 --> 00:08:13.640
and connect that to the web,
connect that to the internet.

00:08:13.640 --> 00:08:18.160
And so basically, you
listen for HTTP traffic,

00:08:18.160 --> 00:08:20.820
and Cloud Run handles
the rest of it.

00:08:20.820 --> 00:08:22.740
It does SSL termination for you.

00:08:22.740 --> 00:08:25.470
It does the actual
traffic routing

00:08:25.470 --> 00:08:27.237
to the different processes.

00:08:27.237 --> 00:08:28.070
So it might run one.

00:08:28.070 --> 00:08:32.220
It might run 50 to handle the
amount of traffic you have.

00:08:32.220 --> 00:08:37.200
And, in fact, fairly
uncommonly, it

00:08:37.200 --> 00:08:40.480
can actually send multiple
requests to the same process.

00:08:40.480 --> 00:08:43.140
So if you've got a process
written in Java, Go, what

00:08:43.140 --> 00:08:46.260
have you, where it can handle
multiple concurrent requests

00:08:46.260 --> 00:08:48.930
at the same time, you
can do that in Cloud Run.

00:08:48.930 --> 00:08:52.650
And since you're billed on
while that process is actually

00:08:52.650 --> 00:08:56.910
running, you can handle multiple
requests in the same process

00:08:56.910 --> 00:08:57.910
and save money.

00:08:57.910 --> 00:08:59.220
So that's really nice.

00:08:59.220 --> 00:09:01.740
It scales up and
down very quickly,

00:09:01.740 --> 00:09:05.060
and very granular
[? billing ?] on usage.

00:09:05.060 --> 00:09:08.940
So when you're writing
something in Cloud Run,

00:09:08.940 --> 00:09:12.552
you are thinking about
building out that whole image--

00:09:12.552 --> 00:09:14.260
the software that runs
in it, which files

00:09:14.260 --> 00:09:17.567
are there, that sort of
thing-- and then, also, it's

00:09:17.567 --> 00:09:20.150
kind of to you to figure out how
you want to build that image.

00:09:20.150 --> 00:09:23.170
So anything from doing it as
a one-off on your computer

00:09:23.170 --> 00:09:25.720
or, more likely,
using a build system

00:09:25.720 --> 00:09:30.250
of some kind that then pushes
the resulting docker image up

00:09:30.250 --> 00:09:31.660
to a repository.

00:09:31.660 --> 00:09:33.730
And then, when you
set up Cloud Run,

00:09:33.730 --> 00:09:35.920
you basically say, pull
from this repository

00:09:35.920 --> 00:09:38.020
and run the
processes from there.

00:09:38.020 --> 00:09:41.332
And the contract
is pretty simple.

00:09:41.332 --> 00:09:42.790
You look at an
environment variable

00:09:42.790 --> 00:09:45.970
for what port to listen on,
and you start a web server

00:09:45.970 --> 00:09:48.470
and do work.

00:09:48.470 --> 00:09:51.400
And then, the other
kind of constraint

00:09:51.400 --> 00:09:53.860
is that there's no
local persistent storage

00:09:53.860 --> 00:09:54.980
for that process.

00:09:54.980 --> 00:09:58.000
So this lets us scale up and
down, [INAUDIBLE] processes,

00:09:58.000 --> 00:09:59.740
start processes,
different places,

00:09:59.740 --> 00:10:03.250
and handle lots of
traffic for you.

00:10:03.250 --> 00:10:07.950
So that contract's
not super onerous.

00:10:07.950 --> 00:10:10.110
It's not a super big deal.

00:10:10.110 --> 00:10:15.120
So that means you can run
nearly any software that

00:10:15.120 --> 00:10:18.730
runs in a container, and run
that in this very scalable way.

00:10:18.730 --> 00:10:21.380
So it gives you a
lot of flexibility.

00:10:21.380 --> 00:10:23.970
It's a really good
fit for workloads

00:10:23.970 --> 00:10:27.120
that are inherently
stateless, for things

00:10:27.120 --> 00:10:30.390
that are kind of web,
HTTP request response,

00:10:30.390 --> 00:10:33.240
and, in particular, where you
have specific dependencies,

00:10:33.240 --> 00:10:36.660
needs, you need to run a
certain programming language.

00:10:36.660 --> 00:10:39.810
If you want to run something
written in COBOL or Fortran

00:10:39.810 --> 00:10:41.610
and put it on the
web on Google Cloud,

00:10:41.610 --> 00:10:44.010
this is going to be a
really good place to start.

00:10:44.010 --> 00:10:48.073
Or if you've got
really particular

00:10:48.073 --> 00:10:49.490
module dependencies--
maybe you've

00:10:49.490 --> 00:10:52.160
got a Python module that
needs a library written in C

00:10:52.160 --> 00:10:53.720
and needs to work a certain way.

00:10:53.720 --> 00:10:56.510
This is a good
place to run that.

00:10:56.510 --> 00:10:58.423
On the constraint
side, you do have

00:10:58.423 --> 00:11:00.590
to think about and use
containers, so it's up to you

00:11:00.590 --> 00:11:04.160
to figure out how you
want to do that build.

00:11:04.160 --> 00:11:06.330
And then, in addition
to what I just

00:11:06.330 --> 00:11:09.420
talked about with the fully
managed portion of Cloud Run,

00:11:09.420 --> 00:11:12.690
there's also the
same exact contract

00:11:12.690 --> 00:11:14.490
can be supported on
top of Kubernetes.

00:11:14.490 --> 00:11:16.800
So you can then--

00:11:16.800 --> 00:11:18.900
anything you write
to run in Cloud Run

00:11:18.900 --> 00:11:20.610
can run anywhere
Kubernetes runs,

00:11:20.610 --> 00:11:23.340
so that can be on Google
Cloud or another Cloud

00:11:23.340 --> 00:11:25.170
or on your own
computers-- wherever

00:11:25.170 --> 00:11:26.640
you've got Kubernetes going.

00:11:26.640 --> 00:11:32.850
So that's really powerful,
which brings us to Kubernetes.

00:11:32.850 --> 00:11:36.270
And Kubernetes itself is
an open source project.

00:11:36.270 --> 00:11:41.070
It's essentially a de facto
software for running containers

00:11:41.070 --> 00:11:42.750
at this point.

00:11:42.750 --> 00:11:44.473
I think of it as kind of--

00:11:44.473 --> 00:11:45.890
when you're like,
hey, containers.

00:11:45.890 --> 00:11:47.270
That runs on my machine thing.

00:11:47.270 --> 00:11:48.510
That's a good idea.

00:11:48.510 --> 00:11:49.400
Let's do a lot of it.

00:11:49.400 --> 00:11:51.350
And you end up, pretty
quickly, with thousands

00:11:51.350 --> 00:11:52.883
and tens of thousands
of containers,

00:11:52.883 --> 00:11:54.800
and you want to figure
out how to manage them.

00:11:54.800 --> 00:11:57.230
That's the problem
space that Kubernetes

00:11:57.230 --> 00:11:59.510
was built to help you with.

00:11:59.510 --> 00:12:00.480
So it helps with that.

00:12:00.480 --> 00:12:04.400
It helps you insulate yourself
from different physical

00:12:04.400 --> 00:12:05.573
environments of computers.

00:12:05.573 --> 00:12:08.240
And then I think it really helps
with a mental model of thinking

00:12:08.240 --> 00:12:10.670
about an application, instead
of how the computers talk

00:12:10.670 --> 00:12:12.330
to each other.

00:12:12.330 --> 00:12:15.930
And then, Kubernetes Engine is
the Google Cloud hosted version

00:12:15.930 --> 00:12:16.920
of Kubernetes.

00:12:16.920 --> 00:12:19.500
So we handle all
the machine updates.

00:12:19.500 --> 00:12:22.800
We've made it really easy
to get the cluster set up

00:12:22.800 --> 00:12:26.290
and get connectivity to it,
help you scale up and down,

00:12:26.290 --> 00:12:27.710
things like that.

00:12:27.710 --> 00:12:29.210
So when you're using
Kubernetes, you

00:12:29.210 --> 00:12:32.797
are thinking about the
logical infrastructure.

00:12:32.797 --> 00:12:35.380
And I like to think about this
as kind of like the whiteboard.

00:12:35.380 --> 00:12:37.140
So when you and a
colleague get together

00:12:37.140 --> 00:12:40.540
and you start drawing lines
and boxes on the board

00:12:40.540 --> 00:12:42.930
to explain how things
work, Kubernetes actually

00:12:42.930 --> 00:12:44.610
works pretty close
to that level.

00:12:44.610 --> 00:12:47.010
So things that would
be a box and a line--

00:12:47.010 --> 00:12:48.510
you're going to
make a configuration

00:12:48.510 --> 00:12:50.590
file that represents that--

00:12:50.590 --> 00:12:53.140
[INAUDIBLE] file
somewhere to do that.

00:12:53.140 --> 00:12:56.020
And so you're really thinking
at that level of abstraction,

00:12:56.020 --> 00:12:58.760
and it's really powerful.

00:12:58.760 --> 00:12:59.917
So what are the programs?

00:12:59.917 --> 00:13:00.750
How do they connect?

00:13:00.750 --> 00:13:02.330
Where do they source data?

00:13:02.330 --> 00:13:03.600
How do they find each other?

00:13:03.600 --> 00:13:07.620
What are they allowed to
do-- things like that.

00:13:07.620 --> 00:13:11.700
It's a particularly good fit
for anytime you have a complex

00:13:11.700 --> 00:13:14.112
software setup-- micro
services, or just, like,

00:13:14.112 --> 00:13:16.570
half a dozen pieces that are
trying to talk to each other--

00:13:16.570 --> 00:13:18.653
and you need to run that
in multiple environments.

00:13:18.653 --> 00:13:20.522
So if you need to
run it in a test

00:13:20.522 --> 00:13:22.230
environment, a
[? fraught ?] environment,

00:13:22.230 --> 00:13:25.170
and have it work the same
way, or in different legal

00:13:25.170 --> 00:13:27.690
environments where you have--

00:13:27.690 --> 00:13:30.960
your main instance of the
cluster maybe runs somewhere,

00:13:30.960 --> 00:13:34.110
and then you need to run another
instance in another country

00:13:34.110 --> 00:13:36.810
because they have specific rules
around that your company needs

00:13:36.810 --> 00:13:38.130
to follow.

00:13:38.130 --> 00:13:43.710
So you can write the code
and the management work,

00:13:43.710 --> 00:13:46.880
and do it in the same way
in both those environments.

00:13:46.880 --> 00:13:48.200
So that's really powerful.

00:13:48.200 --> 00:13:50.110
And it grew up with containers.

00:13:50.110 --> 00:13:52.650
It's all about helping
you run containers.

00:13:52.650 --> 00:13:55.560
So if you and your
team is focused

00:13:55.560 --> 00:13:58.320
on using containers and
getting the most out of them,

00:13:58.320 --> 00:14:00.840
this is probably a
really good fit for that.

00:14:00.840 --> 00:14:04.160
And related to that,
though, I think

00:14:04.160 --> 00:14:05.910
it's all in on
containers, so it really

00:14:05.910 --> 00:14:09.160
means you need
good communication

00:14:09.160 --> 00:14:11.230
between the different
teams and the roles.

00:14:11.230 --> 00:14:15.090
So your dev folks and
your operations folks

00:14:15.090 --> 00:14:18.340
and your security folks all need
access to the same repository

00:14:18.340 --> 00:14:21.340
so that when there is a
security patch that comes out,

00:14:21.340 --> 00:14:24.075
they can patch the same
place that the development

00:14:24.075 --> 00:14:26.200
work is happening so that
it gets out to production

00:14:26.200 --> 00:14:28.120
the same way--

00:14:28.120 --> 00:14:29.680
that sort of thing.

00:14:29.680 --> 00:14:32.500
And that gets into the
constraints a little bit.

00:14:32.500 --> 00:14:35.500
So you need to be
using containers.

00:14:35.500 --> 00:14:37.450
It's up to you to
figure out how you're

00:14:37.450 --> 00:14:40.700
going to coordinate
the builds of those,

00:14:40.700 --> 00:14:42.860
and how your teams are
going to work together.

00:14:42.860 --> 00:14:45.130
And then there can
be, occasionally, some

00:14:45.130 --> 00:14:48.968
challenges around licensing
or certain architectures,

00:14:48.968 --> 00:14:51.010
but around licensing, in
particular, because it's

00:14:51.010 --> 00:14:52.850
a pool of machines.

00:14:52.850 --> 00:14:54.610
Some licenses want to
be machine specific,

00:14:54.610 --> 00:14:57.140
and so there's some
options for doing that,

00:14:57.140 --> 00:14:59.718
but you have to do a little
bit more configuration.

00:15:02.732 --> 00:15:04.190
All of that is true
of Kubernetes--

00:15:04.190 --> 00:15:07.308
all of these things-- this
insulation and abstraction--

00:15:07.308 --> 00:15:08.100
that sort of thing.

00:15:08.100 --> 00:15:11.245
The other point I just wanted
to make is that, I think,

00:15:11.245 --> 00:15:13.370
part of the reason anybody
cares about containers--

00:15:13.370 --> 00:15:14.890
and Kubernetes, as a whole--

00:15:14.890 --> 00:15:16.640
a big one that's not
talked about too much

00:15:16.640 --> 00:15:18.320
is that it helps you save money.

00:15:18.320 --> 00:15:20.720
You can run more software
on the same computer,

00:15:20.720 --> 00:15:23.840
or virtual machine,
without it messing up

00:15:23.840 --> 00:15:25.880
the other software
on the same computer

00:15:25.880 --> 00:15:28.140
because the containers
insulate them from each other.

00:15:28.140 --> 00:15:30.057
And that means you can
get higher utilization.

00:15:30.057 --> 00:15:32.390
You get more work done for
the same amount of money.

00:15:32.390 --> 00:15:34.650
So that's really valuable.

00:15:34.650 --> 00:15:37.250
And then, Kubernetes
Engine itself

00:15:37.250 --> 00:15:40.790
gets you all that and, with
more management of the cluster

00:15:40.790 --> 00:15:43.430
itself, as one big advantage.

00:15:43.430 --> 00:15:45.380
And the other is
sometimes it's not clear

00:15:45.380 --> 00:15:48.000
whether Kubernetes is going
to be a good fit for you,

00:15:48.000 --> 00:15:49.917
or you want to learn it,
or whether it's going

00:15:49.917 --> 00:15:51.200
to work on a given project.

00:15:51.200 --> 00:15:55.700
And a managed cluster is just
the easiest way to get started.

00:15:55.700 --> 00:15:57.650
You can have a cluster
going in five minutes

00:15:57.650 --> 00:15:59.298
and then play with Kubernetes.

00:15:59.298 --> 00:16:01.340
Even if you don't actually
intend to run it there

00:16:01.340 --> 00:16:03.548
long term, it's going be
way easier to get it started

00:16:03.548 --> 00:16:07.430
and figure out whether it's
a good fit for your problem.

00:16:07.430 --> 00:16:09.310
So I recommend that.

00:16:09.310 --> 00:16:12.230
Next up is Compute Engine.

00:16:12.230 --> 00:16:14.323
So these are virtual
machines, and I'm

00:16:14.323 --> 00:16:15.740
going to come back
to this phrase,

00:16:15.740 --> 00:16:19.440
but I like to think about it
as a slice of a data center.

00:16:19.440 --> 00:16:23.400
So basically, it's a
computer, and you've

00:16:23.400 --> 00:16:28.187
got a certain amount of memory,
disk, and network and CPU.

00:16:28.187 --> 00:16:30.270
The thing that makes our
general purpose machines,

00:16:30.270 --> 00:16:33.120
in particular, really
awesome is that you basically

00:16:33.120 --> 00:16:36.420
have a separate dial as an
option on each of those things.

00:16:36.420 --> 00:16:38.880
You can attach multiple disks,
or different sized disks,

00:16:38.880 --> 00:16:40.950
to any of the size machines.

00:16:40.950 --> 00:16:45.090
You can have machines that
have more memory or more CPU,

00:16:45.090 --> 00:16:47.040
depending upon the
actual workload you have.

00:16:47.040 --> 00:16:48.900
So basically, you
don't have to pay

00:16:48.900 --> 00:16:50.970
for the part of the
computer you're not using.

00:16:50.970 --> 00:16:55.290
And it makes it easier,
especially for beefier systems,

00:16:55.290 --> 00:16:59.190
to make a machine that just
matches the kind of work

00:16:59.190 --> 00:17:00.690
that machine is doing.

00:17:00.690 --> 00:17:04.319
You get the most efficiency
and not anymore, I guess--

00:17:04.319 --> 00:17:05.625
not wasting anything.

00:17:05.625 --> 00:17:07.500
And then we do have a
few specialized machine

00:17:07.500 --> 00:17:11.010
types that are extra
large in either memory,

00:17:11.010 --> 00:17:12.390
in particular,
for running things

00:17:12.390 --> 00:17:14.700
like SAP HANA in
memory databases,

00:17:14.700 --> 00:17:19.930
or for doing really
compute-intensive workloads.

00:17:19.930 --> 00:17:22.339
So take a look at those.

00:17:22.339 --> 00:17:24.140
And then, as a whole,
all of these-- they

00:17:24.140 --> 00:17:25.530
start very quickly.

00:17:25.530 --> 00:17:28.010
And we have a lot of
different operating systems

00:17:28.010 --> 00:17:32.870
that are part of Compute Engine
that we do redistributions

00:17:32.870 --> 00:17:34.790
of that have a few
extra tools in them

00:17:34.790 --> 00:17:36.390
to make it really easy to run.

00:17:36.390 --> 00:17:40.730
So that's Debian, CentOS,
CoreOS, Ubuntu, Red Hat, SUSE,

00:17:40.730 --> 00:17:43.760
and, of course, Windows
Server, different editions.

00:17:43.760 --> 00:17:45.420
So take a look at those.

00:17:45.420 --> 00:17:48.770
And if you need to, you can
also roll your own distribution,

00:17:48.770 --> 00:17:52.820
or build a custom organization
specific version of one

00:17:52.820 --> 00:17:56.605
of these and resave that.

00:17:56.605 --> 00:17:58.355
And I want to talk
about Disk for a moment

00:17:58.355 --> 00:17:59.960
because that's
where I think this--

00:17:59.960 --> 00:18:02.930
we normally think of a VM
as a slice of one computer.

00:18:02.930 --> 00:18:06.388
And our Disks, and
network in particular,

00:18:06.388 --> 00:18:08.930
are really-- you're getting a
slice of the whole data center.

00:18:08.930 --> 00:18:10.700
The default disks,
the persistent disks,

00:18:10.700 --> 00:18:12.440
are network attached
storage, but they're

00:18:12.440 --> 00:18:14.840
on really fast networks, and
the blocks are spread out

00:18:14.840 --> 00:18:17.540
across thousands of machines.

00:18:17.540 --> 00:18:19.550
So you get most of
what you would get out

00:18:19.550 --> 00:18:22.400
of SAN for every disk.

00:18:22.400 --> 00:18:24.650
So things like you can
take an image of the disk

00:18:24.650 --> 00:18:25.790
while it's running.

00:18:25.790 --> 00:18:27.860
Restore that onto another
computer to look at it.

00:18:27.860 --> 00:18:30.310
You can make any
of the disks larger

00:18:30.310 --> 00:18:31.760
while the computer's running.

00:18:31.760 --> 00:18:33.920
You can have disks
that are replicated

00:18:33.920 --> 00:18:37.160
and actually live in one region,
spread across multiple failure

00:18:37.160 --> 00:18:38.150
zones.

00:18:38.150 --> 00:18:41.330
Things like that
really help out a lot,

00:18:41.330 --> 00:18:43.760
and can drop whole parts
of your architecture

00:18:43.760 --> 00:18:48.220
out, make things easier
to run and operate.

00:18:48.220 --> 00:18:50.680
And then, they're more
than just computers.

00:18:50.680 --> 00:18:54.070
So there's this handy thing
you can do where if you just

00:18:54.070 --> 00:18:55.270
want to run one VM--

00:18:55.270 --> 00:18:58.060
or one container on a
VM, you can just do that,

00:18:58.060 --> 00:19:00.610
and we'll handle starting the
OS up and running the container

00:19:00.610 --> 00:19:01.360
and downloading it.

00:19:01.360 --> 00:19:03.130
And you just don't
have to worry about it.

00:19:03.130 --> 00:19:05.710
And that's super handy if you
need a really big container,

00:19:05.710 --> 00:19:09.430
for example, or you
just don't want to mess

00:19:09.430 --> 00:19:12.740
with the complexity of
running a larger system.

00:19:12.740 --> 00:19:15.100
And then, on top of
all the VMs, there's

00:19:15.100 --> 00:19:18.740
this API called Managed
Instance Groups,

00:19:18.740 --> 00:19:21.500
and it lets you treat a whole
group of machines as one.

00:19:21.500 --> 00:19:24.850
So you have a template
for the OS disk,

00:19:24.850 --> 00:19:27.730
and there's a dial, basically,
for how many copies of that

00:19:27.730 --> 00:19:29.140
you'd like to run at any time.

00:19:29.140 --> 00:19:31.510
So you can say, OK, I
want more, or I want less,

00:19:31.510 --> 00:19:33.610
and we'll start up the
VMs, handle the load

00:19:33.610 --> 00:19:37.060
balancing, and all that sort
of stuff, and connect them all

00:19:37.060 --> 00:19:38.440
and have that work.

00:19:38.440 --> 00:19:40.450
And then, in addition,
you can set up

00:19:40.450 --> 00:19:42.970
to be automatic, so that
it scales based on traffic,

00:19:42.970 --> 00:19:44.890
or you can run different
versions of the app

00:19:44.890 --> 00:19:47.050
and see how they're doing--

00:19:47.050 --> 00:19:49.370
things like that.

00:19:49.370 --> 00:19:52.560
And then, the load balancer
itself is actually also

00:19:52.560 --> 00:19:54.940
a data center level construct.

00:19:54.940 --> 00:19:58.840
So when you change the
config of the load balancer,

00:19:58.840 --> 00:20:01.540
you're actually changing
the configuration

00:20:01.540 --> 00:20:02.650
for the whole data center.

00:20:02.650 --> 00:20:04.960
And the same load
balancers we use for Gmail

00:20:04.960 --> 00:20:08.520
and for YouTube are
serving your traffic,

00:20:08.520 --> 00:20:11.080
and so it's not going through
one particular box somewhere.

00:20:11.080 --> 00:20:12.790
So it can scale up really fast--

00:20:12.790 --> 00:20:15.650
not limit the traffic.

00:20:15.650 --> 00:20:18.380
So when you're using VMs, you're
thinking about everything,

00:20:18.380 --> 00:20:18.880
right?

00:20:18.880 --> 00:20:20.530
You're thinking
about your software.

00:20:20.530 --> 00:20:22.335
You're thinking about
the operating system,

00:20:22.335 --> 00:20:23.710
the disks you're
making for that,

00:20:23.710 --> 00:20:26.500
the configuration of the
machine, how much CPU,

00:20:26.500 --> 00:20:28.930
RAM, and disk, the
networking, how

00:20:28.930 --> 00:20:32.110
they talk to each other,
firewalls, load balancing, all

00:20:32.110 --> 00:20:33.620
of it.

00:20:33.620 --> 00:20:35.120
So it's a lot, but
at the same time,

00:20:35.120 --> 00:20:37.707
it gives you the flexibility
to run pretty much anything

00:20:37.707 --> 00:20:38.540
that already exists.

00:20:38.540 --> 00:20:40.310
So if you have an
existing system,

00:20:40.310 --> 00:20:43.510
it's probably going
to be where it fits.

00:20:43.510 --> 00:20:44.010
Yeah.

00:20:44.010 --> 00:20:47.485
On that note, it's
like lift and shift--

00:20:47.485 --> 00:20:49.152
a system that's
already running--

00:20:49.152 --> 00:20:50.610
this is probably
where you're going

00:20:50.610 --> 00:20:53.490
to want to start running that.

00:20:53.490 --> 00:20:55.080
That use case I
talked about for,

00:20:55.080 --> 00:20:58.140
like, I just want to run a
container-- really handy.

00:20:58.140 --> 00:21:00.900
Also, if you have very
particular licensing

00:21:00.900 --> 00:21:02.190
requirements--

00:21:02.190 --> 00:21:05.250
it's maybe bound to a
particular machine--

00:21:05.250 --> 00:21:07.050
we can handle that
on Compute Engine.

00:21:07.050 --> 00:21:10.230
Or if you need a particular
kernel, or kernel extension,

00:21:10.230 --> 00:21:13.670
or even a very specific OS--

00:21:13.670 --> 00:21:14.740
maybe even a custom OS--

00:21:14.740 --> 00:21:17.620
all of that can be
done on Compute Engine.

00:21:17.620 --> 00:21:22.945
And it is where, I think, most
people run intensive databases.

00:21:22.945 --> 00:21:24.570
It's running on time
of Compute Engine,

00:21:24.570 --> 00:21:28.260
including our managed Cloud SQL
instances are, in turn, running

00:21:28.260 --> 00:21:30.400
on Compute Engine.

00:21:30.400 --> 00:21:36.810
And you can speak any IP
protocol network service--

00:21:36.810 --> 00:21:39.720
any TCP protocol-based
network service.

00:21:39.720 --> 00:21:44.682
So it's a good fit for a
really wide variety of things.

00:21:44.682 --> 00:21:46.140
On the constraints
side, most of it

00:21:46.140 --> 00:21:48.150
is that you end up having
to think about more.

00:21:48.150 --> 00:21:50.400
And you have to decide how
to handle software updates.

00:21:50.400 --> 00:21:55.270
And adding a new computer
takes, like, 20, 30 seconds.

00:21:55.270 --> 00:21:57.960
So there's a larger
chunk of time

00:21:57.960 --> 00:22:02.100
before you can respond
to new traffic.

00:22:02.100 --> 00:22:02.600
OK.

00:22:02.600 --> 00:22:06.410
So did I answer this question
of where I talk about my code?

00:22:09.200 --> 00:22:12.530
Basically, we went through this
whole abstraction [INAUDIBLE]..

00:22:12.530 --> 00:22:15.050
Things are more abstract
up top, and then kind of

00:22:15.050 --> 00:22:17.380
get more concrete as we go down.

00:22:17.380 --> 00:22:20.260
And each of them
has a sweet spot.

00:22:20.260 --> 00:22:24.250
So if in talking about
that you were like, oh hey,

00:22:24.250 --> 00:22:25.770
this is the one for me, great.

00:22:25.770 --> 00:22:27.277
Go start there.

00:22:27.277 --> 00:22:28.110
Take a look at that.

00:22:28.110 --> 00:22:30.450
Maybe look at the
ones next to it.

00:22:30.450 --> 00:22:32.780
But got it.

00:22:32.780 --> 00:22:33.660
Let's start there.

00:22:33.660 --> 00:22:35.890
Do that.

00:22:35.890 --> 00:22:37.890
But there's-- it
still depends, right?

00:22:37.890 --> 00:22:40.060
And I want to talk
about a few other things

00:22:40.060 --> 00:22:42.320
it might depend on.

00:22:42.320 --> 00:22:45.270
So there's this
abstraction level.

00:22:45.270 --> 00:22:47.520
There's technical
requirements of the software

00:22:47.520 --> 00:22:50.100
you're building, and
then there's your team.

00:22:50.100 --> 00:22:51.300
What are the humans?

00:22:51.300 --> 00:22:52.650
What do they like doing?

00:22:52.650 --> 00:22:54.450
What are they good at doing?

00:22:54.450 --> 00:22:56.040
How do the different
teams get along?

00:22:56.040 --> 00:22:57.910
There are some factors
there to think about.

00:22:57.910 --> 00:22:59.820
So let's go into those.

00:22:59.820 --> 00:23:00.922
So abstraction.

00:23:00.922 --> 00:23:02.880
I think we kind of spent
the most time on this.

00:23:02.880 --> 00:23:08.670
Basically, what are the things
your team likes thinking about?

00:23:08.670 --> 00:23:11.550
What is the best fit for the
problem you're working on?

00:23:11.550 --> 00:23:15.750
And that may drive you to pick
a particular one of these.

00:23:15.750 --> 00:23:17.760
So think about that.

00:23:17.760 --> 00:23:20.580
Additionally, there are
technical requirements

00:23:20.580 --> 00:23:23.340
for the software, and
these, generally, pull you

00:23:23.340 --> 00:23:25.660
down the abstraction hierarchy.

00:23:25.660 --> 00:23:30.690
So if you, for example,
need to use a GPU right now,

00:23:30.690 --> 00:23:35.940
that's going to pull you down
into the lower level items.

00:23:35.940 --> 00:23:40.028
If you need a really
specific Linux kernel,

00:23:40.028 --> 00:23:42.070
that's going to pull you
all the way down to VMs.

00:23:42.070 --> 00:23:44.250
So look at what your
technical requirements are.

00:23:44.250 --> 00:23:48.120
Make sure that they work on
the service you want to use.

00:23:48.120 --> 00:23:51.540
And, occasionally, it may
pull you down a level.

00:23:51.540 --> 00:23:53.940
Billing model I didn't
talk as much about,

00:23:53.940 --> 00:23:59.110
but basically, the top half is
based on transactional usage.

00:23:59.110 --> 00:24:03.030
A request comes in and it
costs a small amount of money

00:24:03.030 --> 00:24:05.250
to serve that request.

00:24:05.250 --> 00:24:06.840
You get that sort
of thing going on.

00:24:06.840 --> 00:24:10.980
At the bottom, we have more of
a while it's on, it costs money.

00:24:10.980 --> 00:24:13.920
So you're making a reservation,
and even if you don't use it,

00:24:13.920 --> 00:24:16.290
it still costs money, so
it's really to your advantage

00:24:16.290 --> 00:24:18.780
to use it all the way--

00:24:18.780 --> 00:24:20.740
get high utilization.

00:24:20.740 --> 00:24:25.020
So how the billing
works for your software,

00:24:25.020 --> 00:24:27.840
and how your organization
thinks about billing,

00:24:27.840 --> 00:24:31.110
that also may
affect the decision.

00:24:31.110 --> 00:24:33.540
And then also the team.

00:24:33.540 --> 00:24:35.670
If your group is
entirely made up

00:24:35.670 --> 00:24:38.943
of developers who don't want
to think about computers at all

00:24:38.943 --> 00:24:40.860
and want to focus on the
code they're writing,

00:24:40.860 --> 00:24:44.160
you're more likely to be
higher up in the abstraction.

00:24:44.160 --> 00:24:47.610
And if you have a kind of
a multi-team group that

00:24:47.610 --> 00:24:50.550
has really particular opinions
about how things should

00:24:50.550 --> 00:24:52.560
be configured and how
they should work together,

00:24:52.560 --> 00:24:55.230
you're much more likely to
be lower in the abstraction

00:24:55.230 --> 00:24:56.290
hierarchy here.

00:24:56.290 --> 00:24:58.620
So take a look at
your team makeup

00:24:58.620 --> 00:25:02.910
and also just what you're
good at, what you like doing.

00:25:02.910 --> 00:25:06.160
That may influence
your decision as well.

00:25:06.160 --> 00:25:07.290
And then also, just--

00:25:07.290 --> 00:25:09.630
how long has this code existed?

00:25:09.630 --> 00:25:12.120
I think, generally,
I recommend if you're

00:25:12.120 --> 00:25:14.022
starting something new,
try to start high up

00:25:14.022 --> 00:25:15.230
in the abstraction hierarchy.

00:25:15.230 --> 00:25:18.000
It's going to save you time.

00:25:18.000 --> 00:25:21.523
It tends to be relatively
easier to move down.

00:25:21.523 --> 00:25:23.190
It's to your advantage
to start and stay

00:25:23.190 --> 00:25:26.010
up as high as you can,
as long as you can,

00:25:26.010 --> 00:25:28.200
whereas if you've
got existing code,

00:25:28.200 --> 00:25:30.853
it's almost always going
to work on Compute Engine,

00:25:30.853 --> 00:25:32.520
and if it's in
containers, it's probably

00:25:32.520 --> 00:25:35.850
going to work on Kubernetes
or maybe even Cloud Run.

00:25:35.850 --> 00:25:38.730
So likely you kind of
start towards the bottom

00:25:38.730 --> 00:25:40.300
and work your way up over time.

00:25:40.300 --> 00:25:41.988
So maybe you migrate
into the Cloud,

00:25:41.988 --> 00:25:44.280
and then you move parts of
the system into other things

00:25:44.280 --> 00:25:45.390
later--

00:25:45.390 --> 00:25:46.330
things like that.

00:25:46.330 --> 00:25:49.510
So think about those things.

00:25:49.510 --> 00:25:54.890
And then, when it comes to,
did I make the right decision--

00:25:54.890 --> 00:25:57.410
actually, realistically,
most systems

00:25:57.410 --> 00:26:00.500
are made up of multiples
of these together.

00:26:00.500 --> 00:26:05.090
So it's pretty common,
for example, to have a--

00:26:05.090 --> 00:26:06.860
it's primarily a
Kubernetes based system,

00:26:06.860 --> 00:26:11.270
but you've got a few existing
apps that run on their own VMs,

00:26:11.270 --> 00:26:13.520
and you've got some glue
that runs in Cloud Functions,

00:26:13.520 --> 00:26:14.700
for example.

00:26:14.700 --> 00:26:18.650
Or you might have any different
set of combinations there.

00:26:18.650 --> 00:26:20.540
So that happens.

00:26:20.540 --> 00:26:24.220
It's common and normal.

00:26:24.220 --> 00:26:26.830
So just pick the right thing
for each part of your system.

00:26:26.830 --> 00:26:28.790
So that's a good option there.

00:26:28.790 --> 00:26:33.200
And we have a couple tools
to make that easier to do.

00:26:33.200 --> 00:26:35.165
And as you get
more moving parts,

00:26:35.165 --> 00:26:36.540
they become more
and more useful.

00:26:36.540 --> 00:26:41.660
So Pub/Sub is our durable
and high throughput message

00:26:41.660 --> 00:26:42.890
queuing system.

00:26:42.890 --> 00:26:45.480
So you can pass messages
around to different parts

00:26:45.480 --> 00:26:47.720
of the system without
them necessarily being up,

00:26:47.720 --> 00:26:49.490
or you know about them.

00:26:49.490 --> 00:26:51.320
And Cloud Storage
is an object store.

00:26:51.320 --> 00:26:53.863
It's a key value store that
lets you save information

00:26:53.863 --> 00:26:56.030
so that some other part of
the system, or you later,

00:26:56.030 --> 00:26:57.390
can pick it up again.

00:26:57.390 --> 00:26:59.702
So for the stateless
parts of the systems,

00:26:59.702 --> 00:27:01.160
if you're using
lots of serverless,

00:27:01.160 --> 00:27:04.770
chances are you'll store things
longer term in Cloud Storage--

00:27:04.770 --> 00:27:07.350
things like that.

00:27:07.350 --> 00:27:10.130
Or you may decide to move up or
down these abstraction levels

00:27:10.130 --> 00:27:11.910
as you learn more
about your application.

00:27:11.910 --> 00:27:14.790
So you may start up
high and then later

00:27:14.790 --> 00:27:17.432
be like, actually, this
is already in a container.

00:27:17.432 --> 00:27:18.890
We're going to move
it down a level

00:27:18.890 --> 00:27:21.080
so that we can get
access to something

00:27:21.080 --> 00:27:22.190
a little more specific.

00:27:22.190 --> 00:27:26.180
Or we're adding a piece
to the system that

00:27:26.180 --> 00:27:29.000
has a new technical constraint,
and that might pull you down.

00:27:29.000 --> 00:27:31.540
Or you might be
starting down low

00:27:31.540 --> 00:27:34.720
and decide you want to get
some of the management benefits

00:27:34.720 --> 00:27:35.840
to move you up a level.

00:27:35.840 --> 00:27:40.430
So we try to make it relatively
easy to move up and down.

00:27:40.430 --> 00:27:41.990
Once you're in
containers, it gets

00:27:41.990 --> 00:27:44.237
relatively easy to do that.

00:27:44.237 --> 00:27:46.070
It's almost always just
very straightforward

00:27:46.070 --> 00:27:47.625
to go down a level.

00:27:47.625 --> 00:27:49.250
Sometimes it takes
a little bit of work

00:27:49.250 --> 00:27:52.190
to move up an abstraction
level, but that's definitely

00:27:52.190 --> 00:27:53.112
an option.

00:27:53.112 --> 00:27:55.070
And then we try to make
the behavior as similar

00:27:55.070 --> 00:27:57.112
as possible between these
different environments.

00:27:57.112 --> 00:27:59.480
So there's a
metadata server that

00:27:59.480 --> 00:28:02.030
is available in each of
these that you can get things

00:28:02.030 --> 00:28:04.310
like security tokens from.

00:28:04.310 --> 00:28:07.790
You can log into Stackdriver
and do monitoring and learning

00:28:07.790 --> 00:28:09.497
the same way, using
the same code,

00:28:09.497 --> 00:28:11.580
whether it's running on
any of these environments.

00:28:11.580 --> 00:28:15.320
So try to make it pretty
easy to get back and forth.

00:28:15.320 --> 00:28:19.010
And that brings us back to
our high level thing here.

00:28:19.010 --> 00:28:24.530
So these, I think, are,
again, the sweet spots.

00:28:24.530 --> 00:28:28.460
Things overlap a bit, so you
have a choice between two

00:28:28.460 --> 00:28:30.410
or three of these sometimes.

00:28:30.410 --> 00:28:34.300
But I think these are like
what I'd like you to remember.

00:28:34.300 --> 00:28:36.938
If you've got an app that
looks like it's just a web app,

00:28:36.938 --> 00:28:38.480
think about starting
with App Engine.

00:28:38.480 --> 00:28:40.100
If you've got an
existing system,

00:28:40.100 --> 00:28:42.183
probably think about
starting with Compute Engine,

00:28:42.183 --> 00:28:43.650
that sort of thing.

00:28:43.650 --> 00:28:45.440
And if you'd like to
get some more info,

00:28:45.440 --> 00:28:48.890
here's some links and URLs
to go read a bunch more.

00:28:48.890 --> 00:28:51.480
There's some quick starts for
trying things out and the like.

00:28:51.480 --> 00:28:54.350
And my Twitter handle
is briandorsey,

00:28:54.350 --> 00:28:57.060
and feel free to
email me at Google.

00:28:57.060 --> 00:28:58.940
Thank you very, very
much for your time.

00:28:58.940 --> 00:29:00.520
Take care.

